1️⃣ Spring Boot Core & Fundamentals — ANSWERS What is Spring Boot? Spring Boot is an opinionated framework built on top of Spring that simplifies application setup by providing auto-configuration, embedded servers, and starter dependencies so developers can focus on business logic instead of boilerplate.

Difference between Spring and Spring Boot? Spring is a framework that requires manual configuration. Spring Boot reduces configuration by: Auto-configuring beans Providing embedded Tomcat Using starter dependencies Eliminating XML configs

What are starter dependencies? Starters are curated dependency bundles like spring-boot-starter-web that pull in all required libraries with compatible versions.

What is auto-configuration? Auto-configuration automatically configures beans based on: Classpath dependencies Application properties Existing beans Example: If spring-webmvc is on the classpath, Spring auto-configures DispatcherServlet.

What does @SpringBootApplication do? It combines: @Configuration @EnableAutoConfiguration @ComponentScan

Difference between @Component, @Service, @Repository? They’re functionally the same but semantically different: @Component: generic bean @Service: business logic @Repository: data access + exception translation

Controller vs RestController? @Controller → returns views @RestController → returns JSON (adds @ResponseBody)

What is Dependency Injection? DI allows objects to receive dependencies externally rather than creating them internally. It improves testability, decoupling, and maintainability. Constructor injection is preferred because: Makes dependencies explicit Easier testing Immutable fields

2️⃣ REST APIs & Controllers — ANSWERS Difference between GET, POST, PUT, PATCH? GET → fetch data POST → create resource PUT → replace entire resource (idempotent) PATCH → partial update

Path variable vs query param? Path variable → identifies resource (/users/{id}) Query param → filtering/sorting (/users?page=1)

What is ResponseEntity? ResponseEntity allows full control over: HTTP status Headers Response body

What is idempotency? An operation is idempotent if repeating it produces the same result. PUT and DELETE are idempotent POST is not

How do you version APIs? URL versioning: /api/v1/users Header versioning Media type versioning URL versioning is simplest and most common.

What happens when an exception occurs? Without handling → 500 error With global handling → controlled error response via @ControllerAdvice

3️⃣ Spring Data JPA — ANSWERS What is JPA? JPA is a Java specification for ORM that maps Java objects to database tables.

What is Hibernate? Hibernate is the most popular JPA implementation.

CrudRepository vs JpaRepository? CrudRepository → basic CRUD JpaRepository → CRUD + pagination + sorting + batch ops

What does @Entity do? Marks a class as a persistent JPA entity mapped to a table.

Lazy vs Eager loading? Lazy → fetch when accessed (default for collections) Eager → fetch immediately Lazy is preferred to avoid performance issues.

What is the N+1 problem? Occurs when fetching a collection lazily causes one query per child entity. Solved using: Fetch joins Entity graphs Batch fetching

What does @Transactional do? Ensures a method runs within a database transaction. Commits on success Rolls back on runtime exceptions

Why not put @Transactional on controllers? Controllers should handle HTTP, not business logic. Transactions belong in the service layer.

4️⃣ Exception Handling & Validation — ANSWERS How do you handle exceptions globally? Using @ControllerAdvice with @ExceptionHandler.

What is @ControllerAdvice? A centralized exception handling mechanism across all controllers.

What is @Valid? Triggers bean validation on request bodies using annotations like: @NotNull @Size @Email

What happens if validation fails? Spring throws MethodArgumentNotValidException, which should be handled globally.

5️⃣ Configuration & Profiles — ANSWERS application.yml vs properties? YAML is hierarchical and more readable. Functionally identical.

What are Spring Profiles? Profiles allow environment-specific configuration (dev, test, prod).

How do you manage secrets? Environment variables Vault / Secrets Manager Never commit secrets to Git

@Value vs @ConfigurationProperties? @Value → single value @ConfigurationProperties → group related configs (preferred)

6️⃣ Spring Security — ANSWERS (VERY IMPORTANT) Authentication vs Authorization? Authentication → who you are Authorization → what you can do

How does JWT work? User logs in Server issues signed JWT Client sends JWT in Authorization header Server validates token per request

What is OncePerRequestFilter? A filter that runs once per request, typically used for JWT validation.

How do you store passwords? Using BCrypt hashing. Never store plaintext passwords.

What is CSRF? Cross-Site Request Forgery. Disabled for stateless APIs using JWT.

How do you implement role-based access? Using: @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")

7️⃣ Performance & Scalability — ANSWERS Is Spring Boot single-threaded? No. It uses a thread pool (Tomcat/Netty) to handle concurrent requests.

How does Spring Boot scale? Stateless services Horizontal scaling Load balancers

How do you improve performance? Pagination Caching Connection pooling (HikariCP) Avoid N+1 queries

8️⃣ Testing — ANSWERS Unit vs Integration tests? Unit → isolated, fast Integration → full context, slower

What is @SpringBootTest? Loads the full application context for integration testing.

What is @WebMvcTest? Loads only controller layer for fast API testing.

How do you mock dependencies? Using Mockito: @MockBean

9️⃣ Deployment & Production — ANSWERS How is Spring Boot packaged? As a fat JAR with embedded Tomcat.

What are Actuators? Production monitoring endpoints like: /health /metrics

How do you log? Using SLF4J with Logback. Log at appropriate levels (INFO, WARN, ERROR).

